{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-72835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-72835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2.300\\,523…≈2.301\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.301$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.301.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.301 & ≈0.841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.841.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/d02a0250df0f0e6c389594ea0e1b119d9f4c63c353553d56c0e865f487862284.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.841 \\\\  & ≈3.983\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈2.301\\cdot 3.983≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-72835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44fcba5dbef5090a8988c76f31da46093db58469de0203776b08ecd5a0c2965d"} {"question" "q-72835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-72835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-72835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{7}{9}) & =2.461\\,918…≈2.461\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.461\\,9$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.461\\,9.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.461\\,9 & ≈0.679\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.679\\,7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/eafc9be7094c9c0ccdf167cc737d494efec40014a89ad793f7ed34ea5eed94ae.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.679\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.821\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.461\\,9\\cdot 3.821\\,3≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44fcba5dbef5090a8988c76f31da46093db58469de0203776b08ecd5a0c2965d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-120896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-274589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is $x=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-274589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-274589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44fcba5dbef5090a8988c76f31da46093db58469de0203776b08ecd5a0c2965d"} {"question" "q-274589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-274589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/a22dba264faa711352724f135d2ec5a074202c4b5069dee2389fe954419a3770.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44fcba5dbef5090a8988c76f31da46093db58469de0203776b08ecd5a0c2965d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338574" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=2.245\\,927…≈2.246\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.246$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/992325c7cbf34f4b981af0a9f34740990d39694e727861ca92bf591037436327.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.896 \\\\  & ≈4.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.038-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-2.245 \\\\  & ≈-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44fcba5dbef5090a8988c76f31da46093db58469de0203776b08ecd5a0c2965d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"} {"question" "q-338710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d6a6e375aec0d2f6e9daef7d988c025bbe29d9d67279dae9ac41d540c4438d"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1959 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
